    "blurb": "The second set delivers a standout He’s Gone > Other One transition and an expansive Bird Song that stretches over 70 minutes. Early‑version Mississippi Half-Step and Stella Blue provide solid highlights. Recording quality varies, but the marked‑up tape offers the clearest listening experience.",
    "review": "September 19, 1972 at Roosevelt Stadium finds the Dead deep in their classic early‑’70s groove. Set one rolls out a tight mix of roots rock and folk‑rock gems before giving way to an inspired Bird Song that, despite the outdoor weather, still manages to soar. The second set truly shines: He’s Gone segues into a muscular Other One where Jerry’s lead lines cut through the mix, while Phil’s bass drives the improvisation forward. Highlights also include a crisp Mississippi Half‑Step Uptown Toodeloo, a heartfelt Stella Blue, and a rousing finish with Sugar Magnolia, Casey Jones, and an encore of One More Saturday Night. Listeners should gravitate toward the newer marked‑up audience tape (gd1972-09-19.154185.aud.deal.smith.clugston.flac16), which captures the long Bird Song and Other One with far better clarity than earlier sources.",
